1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,743
Hello, sheep.

2
00:00:00,801 --> 00:00:01,415
I'm a wolf.

3
00:00:01,451 --> 00:00:02,553
I work in finance.

4
00:00:02,751 --> 00:00:06,315
And today, I'm going to show you how you lose wool every single year.

5
00:00:06,628 --> 00:00:07,976
Without anyone touching you.

6
00:00:08,243 --> 00:00:09,333
It's called inflation.

7
00:00:09,451 --> 00:00:10,285
And honestly?

8
00:00:10,484 --> 00:00:11,656
It's my favourite haircut.

9
00:00:12,156 --> 00:00:15,789
Stick around, because at the end, I'll tell you exactly who's holding the scissors.

10
00:00:16,139 --> 00:00:17,416
First, the rules.

11
00:00:17,683 --> 00:00:19,459
In this world, wool is wealth.

12
00:00:20,133 --> 00:00:21,712
A fluffy sheep is a rich sheep.

13
00:00:21,828 --> 00:00:24,278
A bald sheep… well.

14
00:00:24,359 --> 00:00:25,114
That's Gary.

15
00:00:25,148 --> 00:00:26,913
You'll be seeing a lot of Gary.

16
00:00:27,263 --> 00:00:29,376
Now, paying with wool is a nightmare.

17
00:00:29,689 --> 00:00:33,474
You walk into a shop, and you pay with a chunk of your own back.

18
00:00:34,055 --> 00:00:35,820
So the King of the sheep had an idea.

19
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:38,884
Give him your wool, and he gives you a receipt.

20
00:00:39,674 --> 00:00:40,440
One receipt.

21
00:00:40,811 --> 00:00:41,845
One bag of wool.

22
00:00:42,518 --> 00:00:43,041
Light.

23
00:00:43,714 --> 00:00:44,236
Clean.

24
00:00:44,353 --> 00:00:45,235
Doesn't itch.

25
00:00:45,815 --> 00:00:46,988
Congratulations, sheep.

26
00:00:47,046 --> 00:00:49,275
You just invented money.

27
00:00:49,626 --> 00:00:51,809
Now, here's the only formula in this whole video.

28
00:00:51,832 --> 00:00:54,363
Price equals money, divided by stuff.

29
00:00:54,676 --> 00:00:57,010
In the whole meadow, there are one hundred receipts.

30
00:00:57,323 --> 00:00:59,309
And one hundred bundles of grass.

31
00:00:59,738 --> 00:01:02,141
So one bundle of grass costs one receipt.

32
00:01:02,814 --> 00:01:03,360
Simple.

33
00:01:03,534 --> 00:01:04,115
Fair.

34
00:01:04,347 --> 00:01:04,939
Boring.

35
00:01:05,171 --> 00:01:06,390
Until the King gets an idea.

36
00:01:06,740 --> 00:01:08,679
This is called monetary inflation.

37
00:01:08,992 --> 00:01:10,397
The King wants a statue.

38
00:01:10,978 --> 00:01:12,603
A giant, golden statue.

39
00:01:12,754 --> 00:01:13,660
Of himself.

40
00:01:14,449 --> 00:01:17,642
But statues are expensive, and he doesn't have enough receipts.

41
00:01:18,222 --> 00:01:19,546
He could raise taxes.

42
00:01:20,219 --> 00:01:21,612
But sheep hate taxes.

43
00:01:21,984 --> 00:01:22,901
He could borrow.

44
00:01:23,575 --> 00:01:24,910
But that's embarrassing.

45
00:01:25,177 --> 00:01:26,419
Then he looks at his stamp.

46
00:01:26,849 --> 00:01:28,950
And he realizes something beautiful.

47
00:01:29,623 --> 00:01:31,249
Sheep take a whole spring to grow.

48
00:01:31,620 --> 00:01:33,072
Receipts take one stamp.

49
00:01:33,571 --> 00:01:35,916
So he stamps one hundred brand new receipts.

50
00:01:36,287 --> 00:01:37,518
No new wool behind them.

51
00:01:38,098 --> 00:01:40,827
Just ink, and optimism.

52
00:01:41,177 --> 00:01:43,174
Now here's the question that really matters.

53
00:01:43,673 --> 00:01:45,368
Who gets the new receipts first?

54
00:01:45,519 --> 00:01:46,309
The builders.

55
00:01:46,460 --> 00:01:47,493
The King's friends.

56
00:01:47,609 --> 00:01:48,387
And the bank.

57
00:01:48,561 --> 00:01:49,443
I am the bank.

58
00:01:49,525 --> 00:01:53,495
I take the new receipts, walk into the shop, and buy grass at the old price.

59
00:01:54,076 --> 00:01:54,970
One receipt.

60
00:01:55,550 --> 00:01:56,502
Lovely.

61
00:01:56,853 --> 00:01:58,316
But now, look at the meadow.

62
00:01:58,548 --> 00:01:59,744
Two hundred receipts.

63
00:01:59,976 --> 00:02:01,845
Still one hundred bundles of grass.

64
00:02:02,112 --> 00:02:06,036
The shopkeeper sees a long line of sheep, all waving receipts.

65
00:02:06,617 --> 00:02:09,043
So she does what any shopkeeper would do.

66
00:02:09,415 --> 00:02:10,297
Two receipts.

67
00:02:10,472 --> 00:02:11,307
Same grass.

68
00:02:11,400 --> 00:02:12,271
Prices double.

69
00:02:12,770 --> 00:02:15,011
That, sheep, is inflation.

70
00:02:15,361 --> 00:02:16,418
And here's the trick.

71
00:02:16,487 --> 00:02:17,451
Watch this sheep.

72
00:02:17,950 --> 00:02:19,517
She earns ten receipts a week.

73
00:02:19,645 --> 00:02:20,736
Nobody cut her pay.

74
00:02:20,829 --> 00:02:21,793
Nobody touched her.

75
00:02:21,944 --> 00:02:23,976
But grass costs twice as much now.

76
00:02:24,405 --> 00:02:26,321
So every week, she can buy less.

77
00:02:27,401 --> 00:02:29,026
Inflation doesn't take your wool.

78
00:02:29,223 --> 00:02:30,756
It makes your wool worth less.

79
00:02:31,069 --> 00:02:32,486
But that's not the worst part.

80
00:02:32,915 --> 00:02:34,889
Grandma saved receipts her whole life.

81
00:02:35,203 --> 00:02:37,873
Overnight, her savings buy half as much grass.

82
00:02:38,372 --> 00:02:39,649
Nobody stole from Grandma.

83
00:02:39,696 --> 00:02:40,950
Technically.

84
00:02:41,299 --> 00:02:42,158
So who wins?

85
00:02:42,205 --> 00:02:43,830
Whoever gets the new money first.

86
00:02:44,062 --> 00:02:45,757
They spend it before prices go up.

87
00:02:46,187 --> 00:02:47,650
Everyone after them pays.

88
00:02:47,824 --> 00:02:49,716
But the statue still isn't finished.

89
00:02:50,390 --> 00:02:54,186
And now the builders want more receipts, because everything costs more.

90
00:02:54,418 --> 00:02:55,707
So the King prints more.

91
00:02:56,021 --> 00:02:56,973
Prices go up.

92
00:02:57,402 --> 00:02:58,157
He prints more.

93
00:02:58,203 --> 00:02:59,248
Prices go up again.

94
00:02:59,376 --> 00:03:02,522
When this spins out of control, it's called hyperinflation.

95
00:03:02,952 --> 00:03:08,141
And that's how a sheep ends up pushing a wheelbarrow of receipts to buy one salad.

96
00:03:08,491 --> 00:03:10,848
But printing isn't the only way to get fleeced.

97
00:03:11,277 --> 00:03:13,077
Sometimes, nobody prints anything.

98
00:03:13,657 --> 00:03:16,618
A storm hits the meadow, and half the grass is gone.

99
00:03:16,931 --> 00:03:17,918
Same receipts.

100
00:03:18,290 --> 00:03:19,265
Half the grass.

101
00:03:19,335 --> 00:03:20,659
Prices double, again.

102
00:03:21,239 --> 00:03:24,443
Economists call this cost-push inflation.

103
00:03:25,024 --> 00:03:25,848
Less stuff.

104
00:03:26,162 --> 00:03:26,951
Same money.

105
00:03:27,265 --> 00:03:28,832
Higher prices.

106
00:03:29,182 --> 00:03:30,587
Meanwhile, in the real world.

107
00:03:30,958 --> 00:03:32,340
You think this is a cartoon?

108
00:03:32,514 --> 00:03:33,234
It's not.

109
00:03:33,408 --> 00:03:35,219
Germany, nineteen twenty-three.

110
00:03:35,718 --> 00:03:37,762
The government printed money to pay its bills.

111
00:03:37,936 --> 00:03:41,558
By November, a single loaf of bread cost around two hundred billion marks.

112
00:03:42,139 --> 00:03:46,841
People were paid twice a day, and ran to the shops before prices changed again.

113
00:03:47,514 --> 00:03:49,290
Zimbabwe, two thousand eight.

114
00:03:49,790 --> 00:03:52,135
Prices were doubling roughly every day.

115
00:03:52,715 --> 00:03:55,258
The country printed a one hundred trillion dollar note.

116
00:03:55,932 --> 00:04:01,539
And in the United States, in June twenty twenty-two, prices were rising at nine point one percent a year.

117
00:04:01,737 --> 00:04:03,269
The highest in about forty years.

118
00:04:03,768 --> 00:04:05,080
Same story every time.

119
00:04:05,394 --> 00:04:06,973
More money, or less stuff.

120
00:04:07,344 --> 00:04:08,935
Higher prices.

121
00:04:09,284 --> 00:04:11,130
So, is all inflation bad?

122
00:04:11,919 --> 00:04:12,616
Not really.

123
00:04:12,674 --> 00:04:14,195
A little is normal.

124
00:04:14,776 --> 00:04:17,260
Many central banks aim for about two percent a year.

125
00:04:17,690 --> 00:04:18,816
Slow, and predictable.

126
00:04:19,396 --> 00:04:21,033
The problem is fast inflation.

127
00:04:21,103 --> 00:04:24,911
Because fast inflation shears the sheep who can least afford it.

128
00:04:25,491 --> 00:04:27,453
So, since I'm in a generous mood.

129
00:04:28,034 --> 00:04:28,498
One.

130
00:04:28,998 --> 00:04:31,784
Cash sitting still slowly loses value.

131
00:04:32,457 --> 00:04:33,711
It's a melting ice cube.

132
00:04:34,640 --> 00:04:35,070
Two.

133
00:04:35,116 --> 00:04:38,181
If your pay doesn't grow with prices, you're getting a haircut.

134
00:04:38,761 --> 00:04:39,760
Ask for a raise.

135
00:04:40,340 --> 00:04:40,851
Three.

136
00:04:41,025 --> 00:04:44,903
Learn how saving and investing work, so your wool grows faster than prices.

137
00:04:45,054 --> 00:04:46,308
Not financial advice.

138
00:04:46,366 --> 00:04:47,596
I'm a wolf.

139
00:04:47,947 --> 00:04:49,154
Oh, and the scissors.

140
00:04:49,305 --> 00:04:50,165
I promised.

141
00:04:50,281 --> 00:04:51,233
Who's holding them?

142
00:04:51,813 --> 00:04:53,404
Whoever gets the new money first.

143
00:04:53,520 --> 00:04:54,112
This time?

144
00:04:54,228 --> 00:04:54,959
That was me.

145
00:04:55,087 --> 00:04:58,559
If you don't want to end up bald like Gary… subscribe.

146
00:04:58,988 --> 00:04:59,928
Gary didn't.
